  "notes": "A Latin-rock songwriter crossing into worldbeat pop: 65–130, and the signature crossover single sits mid-tempo straight with the backbeat on 2&4 and \"a heavy 16th percussion layer\". The modal claim is the harmonic identity and is encoded as written — \"Andean modal colour (natural minor with a flat 7th, no leading tone)\" — so the scales stay minor and no authored family contains a V (`natural_minor` and `aeolian` are one pitch collection — `engine/src/theory.rs` has a test saying so — and the pool names it twice as the shipped `data/genres/rage.json` does, so the two weights sum). Three drum deltas come from the entry: \"kick 1 and the '&' of 3\", \"16th shaker/guiro\" plus congas (the four-lane perc block at 3–6, the highest density in this batch), and \"bombo-style low drum accent on beat 3\", which is the `tomLow` lane; \"percussion break instead of a drum fill\" is why the fill schedule is wide while the percussion stays busy. The bass is \"played bass on roots with an anticipated note before the bar line; octave drops under the chorus\" — a high anticipation with the octave as its passing tone. The instrumental hook is charango, quena and panpipe in baritone register, which is why the countermelody sits an octave *below* the lead. ⚠ `confidence` is medium because the research rates it high-medium and says the tempo band is catalogue-derived; `bpm.mode` 100 is **INFERRED** from the word \"mid-tempo\" rather than from a published figure, as are all drum probabilities.",
